da-86a1-3f28dd5a7353" :question/math-academy-id "q-97064" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/9ba3d1f0eebdfc82c40f36bdbe16896ca15516b623c695b72c331861955329bc.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-97064/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97064"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140362/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "a544c2df-7789-5fb6-9ff2-f639562d61ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140362/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b6452795-8d54-5d