B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140454/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140454"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97111/selection/cc998b8b486bc211be2ed6e65d9e2a77179d5768c57b95f374aa03de1c157fd6" :answer/id #uuid "ca51e8bf-88ba-5394-904f-988e8ddefe74" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 18 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97111/selection/98de0bccf0d661f3b6a6ba4e863376e729865238f72b