.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-97119/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97119"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97278/selection/dd7c609322b9640a03ae937575c4fd3575dda25cbab596efcc001e98a8ed7b41" :answer/id #uuid "b3b07ebc-bf23-5dad-82ab-bc750b5fb656" :answer/type :answer.type/m