#uuid "c615bf54-fc91-5d1c-a18a-12610944ff51" :question/math-academy-id "q-97278" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97278/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97278"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280/selection/ec65ce973d4f496522a07102d3b88d5f3ef38025609ef4c7395d5b11aae1e752" :answer/id #uuid "00381990-0a39-5cfb-8126-d283081d8bbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -13\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280/selection/d44507d24e0c5f9adc12d0085295b532b6e22d30d71e364a95013d492def044a" :answer/id #uuid "de27e708-6a87-55b5-9708-1a7ae9237642" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280/selection/abbcaa3336cf9902907f5b497a1162bee0a608ffeeed51b8ace618fb949fbf14" :answer/id #uuid "482da4b9-18e3-53f9-acc9-3d4625edc8cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280/selection/95ca7e301fef3d3040102874290f0e88375907adb49d2b25a340549006b79fe1" :answer/id #uuid "cf884265-be6c-5778-970a-24ff1fc3578b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280/selection/e47ffd85f85499438ee6be3c02b96ae2a6c549f7b147c86439fbc709fbdff835" :answer/id #uuid "e8ff5875-227f-5201-b483-3adb78931519" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "127f4f10-9e66-52da-8e27-2f21d7414e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97280/selection/95ca7e301fef3d3040102874290f0e88375907adb49d2b25a340549006b79fe1" :answer-field/choices ["q-97280/selection/ec65ce973d4f496522a07102d3b88d5f3ef38025609ef4c7395d5b11aae1e752" "q-97280/selection/d44507d24e0c5f9adc12d0085295b532b6e22d30d71e364a95013d492def044a" "q-97280/selection/abbcaa3336cf9902907f5b497a1162bee0a608ffeeed51b8ace618fb949fbf14" "q-97280/selection/95ca7e301fef3d3040102874290f0e88375907adb49d2b25a340549006b79fe1" "q-97280/selection/e47ffd85f85499438ee6be3c02b96ae2a6c549f7b147c86439fbc709fbdff835"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97280" :question/id #uuid "150207d8-196e-5c9f-bf3a-465dd9f4f1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-97280" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 8 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-11)-4\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97280/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283/selection/fd67e8db47d907026e086ba09edfb4f9bac9e2ec0aaca71194b17aa3a7531c81" :answer/id #uuid "ffd961d8-8219-57af-8ec5-3905df9ccc8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283/selection/4b3e41a948c956161a8544e36b6f1a40e0d7f74921f906324bd2ca2efd07f859" :answer/id #uuid "f7f78628-af29-5273-ab8e-31e0506df01e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283/selection/4d125f17b1771507b9d9afacabf877608d4e73d177c0f0ef695507d3ee9e75aa" :answer/id #uuid "a223c353-5da9-572b-b193-459ce6657910" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283/selection/7cb63935baf4d61ae1756270d4f6ec38a88a15b8b1b88ce20ad168963e2f02ae" :answer/id #uuid "f135668f-ff7c-540b-9a08-9ee64d3cbb86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283/selection/4e828b9e86b59464540c4a51848324bec278582f97a5313a06786a1988a506c1" :answer/id #uuid "55a8f562-43ab-51cd-8e8a-c7dff127c750" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "761b540a-adb6-56c7-a801-13e3d98bfedd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97283/selection/4e828b9e86b59464540c4a51848324bec278582f97a5313a06786a1988a506c1" :answer-field/choices ["q-97283/selection/fd67e8db47d907026e086ba09edfb4f9bac9e2ec0aaca71194b17aa3a7531c81" "q-97283/selection/4b3e41a948c956161a8544e36b6f1a40e0d7f74921f906324bd2ca2efd07f859" "q-97283/selection/4d125f17b1771507b9d9afacabf877608d4e73d177c0f0ef695507d3ee9e75aa" "q-97283/selection/7cb63935baf4d61ae1756270d4f6ec38a88a15b8b1b88ce20ad168963e2f02ae" "q-97283/selection/4e828b9e86b59464540c4a51848324bec278582f97a5313a06786a1988a506c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97283" :question/id #uuid "7c7c77f8-d354-5712-9d15-cc66aa1dd115" :question/math-academy-id "q-97283" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 7 \\\\ 21 & -15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97283/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97283"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281/selection/3e28d8daa43cd7ee154c66f72d4c9b510f8c6931ab7d6007284f92d73f78a0f8" :answer/id #uuid "c1d5af78-73ce-573c-ab16-8882f11b32fe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281/selection/116ba511f78f3ed888236c80aa0db20008860383f2b47eb517f411d99b4bfe21" :answer/id #uuid "a5c02428-046e-57e3-b131-913676e9c806" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281/selection/ccc4dc14e0694bb8f95f4df194a0a6c402681dd31d446c4efa2438918575b1bb" :answer/id #uuid "742022a8-bd35-5eb7-8c5d-7c5631262619" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281/selection/37f90dcabc8c365ace90cafdc22838e6541b80d27ac056f6677684a36f25acf6" :answer/id #uuid "7971f195-b0a6-56d5-a407-d5142d484820" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281/selection/6f5c042bb19dbc8fc5a90af3c70adf4055142e3920edf86687170c59e905f12f" :answer/id #uuid "bb7b5d03-e56a-52a6-9a33-d646a4283ea4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1f4cffae-3058-57f0-9388-bd5b08560299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-97281/selection/6f5c042bb19dbc8fc5a90af3c70adf4055142e3920edf86687170c59e905f12f" :answer-field/choices ["q-97281/selection/3e28d8daa43cd7ee154c66f72d4c9b510f8c6931ab7d6007284f92d73f78a0f8" "q-97281/selection/116ba511f78f3ed888236c80aa0db20008860383f2b47eb517f411d99b4bfe21" "q-97281/selection/ccc4dc14e0694bb8f95f4df194a0a6c402681dd31d446c4efa2438918575b1bb" "q-97281/selection/37f90dcabc8c365ace90cafdc22838e6541b80d27ac056f6677684a36f25acf6" "q-97281/selection/6f5c042bb19dbc8fc5a90af3c70adf4055142e3920edf86687170c59e905f12f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-97281" :question/id #uuid "90dac6d1-6fdb-5236-89cf-8823a20cc32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97281" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-97281/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-97281"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490/selection/f8e015799aecdecb793fc30eac67fc118512fdc9cb6b2d3064832f512cf14184" :answer/id #uuid "a90c4bb2-0cc7-5c76-af03-05fd53a2f555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490/selection/ef30e95f455ea598549c81774ad55b8bb8c5dfa0f851c50e1980d6c8553653e6" :answer/id #uuid "13de4344-9a0a-5382-bab8-0e922ad053a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -11 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490/selection/69f6652b81dbc815a91f6347b578940810e5177ddb9300a01e98c0eee42d0e3e" :answer/id #uuid "db4f1726-7157-59fe-96d8-86c97489d7d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 \\\\ 11 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490/selection/9eae77c773169352e35c6b1f2a76520ddb518040101c3b0f48d17b72d37d8f32" :answer/id #uuid "9cbe2548-39a6-5cd8-a6dd-bc3e01a1e8f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 13 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490/selection/11417763e7fc04f9ad0fdfba5bcee0a2023830c8e1f0c50c7bd488bbb1806d58" :answer/id #uuid "c7e3c671-edb4-5f7b-bef1-4edf4309b945" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "67239114-58f9-55e3-93ff-00aebc565907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140490/selection/f8e015799aecdecb793fc30eac67fc118512fdc9cb6b2d3064832f512cf14184" :answer-field/choices ["q-140490/selection/f8e015799aecdecb793fc30eac67fc118512fdc9cb6b2d3064832f512cf14184" "q-140490/selection/ef30e95f455ea598549c81774ad55b8bb8c5dfa0f851c50e1980d6c8553653e6" "q-140490/selection/69f6652b81dbc815a91f6347b578940810e5177ddb9300a01e98c0eee42d0e3e" "q-140490/selection/9eae77c773169352e35c6b1f2a76520ddb518040101c3b0f48d17b72d37d8f32" "q-140490/selection/11417763e7fc04f9ad0fdfba5bcee0a2023830c8e1f0c50c7bd488bbb1806d58"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140490" :question/id #uuid "1d85c5d1-31cb-5305-85fd-471007b641eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140490" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -11 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140490/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140490"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8563"] :question/problem "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5516"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/48ba234a07831383642525bf18fedcf6ea801576d80702e737d37334a04740e3.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ First, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5517"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/fe3c67c0218808ba7b398d1659530d61a4c8ce3eafd196341ba6645ac7076f43.png)\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/d050ac7d04af79ad9b5e7f096e3d21c9808af4a756f43d46ca29beca00dedcc9.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5897"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/76bfd9209f632ac8576051154d7e4250d8c4c53ffe843238b6d25835704ce398.png)\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/cb0c35da5a4bd26c92343d7262efcb7ba7f197cdeea95c2d541b7441a6485804.png)\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/6759e7c098d5375894d540050e3c3dd9e3bb453b0cac7338b9b6754bb635f717.png)\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
